@misc{DQM25,
  author  = {Betre and Lewis},
  title   = {Dynamical Quantum Multigraphs},
  year    = {2025},
  eprint  = {2509.08296},
  archivePrefix = {arXiv},
  note    = {read-full (2026-09-21, by the owner). Surnames only; initials and given names not yet recorded -- check the arXiv record before citing. **WITHDRAWN CLAIM: the previous note here said this paper shows the transition moving with ln N. It does not.** That came from a third-party review page, not from the paper, and is exactly the failure mode CLAUDE.md warns about. What the paper is actually about: **labelled versus unlabelled vertices.** Labelled means every point has its own identity; unlabelled means points are interchangeable, as identical particles are, so each graph SHAPE counts once. Every run in this repository, and as far as we know every published CQG run, is labelled. Results at N = 10-24: the labelled free model is Erdos-Renyi G(N,p) with an analytic free energy, so no transition; the unlabelled free model and an unlabelled ferromagnetic "graph Ising" model both show transitions (diverging specific heat, critical slowing), with the largest-component fraction as order parameter, and the authors do not settle whether the critical temperature stays finite as N grows; the unlabelled antiferromagnet shows none, because connected graphs almost never have symmetries. Sampling rule for unlabelled graphs, Eq. (72): the Metropolis acceptance is multiplied by |Aut(G')| / |Aut(G)|, which needs the automorphism-group size at every step (nauty or similar) and is expensive. Their ensemble is different from ours -- edges on or off on the complete graph, not regular graphs, and no curvature energy; the "quantum" part is Hilbert-space formalism over classical Boltzmann thermodynamics. Read only Sec. 4 (Eqs. 60-91) and Appendix A; Secs. 2-3 are the Hilbert-space construction. They cite EK25 for a first-order transition in unlabelled random networks that is absent when labelled.}
}

